WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.720
Tissue resident memory t-cells can sense the physical feel of the tissue they occupy, not just the chemical signals around them. That McGill lead result means long-term immunity is not only a chemical conversation. The mechanics of the organ itself can shape the cells that stay behind to protect it. This is Planetterrian Daily, episode 155. These are the t-cells that remain in tissues after an infection has cleared, and respond rapidly if the same threat returns.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:58.640
For years, the field treated that population as a product of cytokines, antigen, and local chemical cues alone. The McGill University led work, reported by Fizz.org, adds a physical dimension. The cells appear able to sense touch, meaning the mechanical character of the tissue can shape them. The finding stops at the existence of that tactile capacity. The receptor, the mechanical cue, and the transduction path are still unnamed.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:18.200
If resident memory can be shaped by how a tissue feels, vaccine and immunotherapy design may have a second control besides the chemical adjuvant. Antigen and the cytokine milieu still matter. So, on this evidence, do the stiffness, stretch, and texture of the organ the cell is asked to occupy?

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:47.480
Tissues that remodel with age, a denser matrix, different mechanics, may not build the same resident memory even when the antigen is identical. That would make part of immune aging a mechanical problem, rather than only a molecular one. The study cannot yet say which tissues are most affected or how to measure the signal in a human biopsy. The next result that matters is how touch is transduced, and whether that pathway can be read or nudged in human tissue.

00:01:47.480 --> 00:02:02.980
Another kind of molecular memory showed up in a separate report, not in the immune system this time, but passed from mother to child. In humans and many other animals, a mother's age can affect the physical and behavioral characteristics of the next generation.

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:14.100
Scientists call these changes maternal age effects, and the pattern is widespread across the animal kingdom. Researchers still do not fully understand how or why it occurs.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:25.320
A new report, also covered by Fizz.org, treats the effect as molecular memory, traces of the mother's age that show up in the offspring cells, not only in the mother.

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:38.660
We covered aging biology yesterday. This does not hand us a human biomarker, naming the molecules that carry that echo, and testing whether they can be read in human tissue is the measurement step still missing.

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:46.880
From a mark the body keeps without asking, we turn to a mark people carry for years, and a method that tries to loosen it.

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:57.180
Childhood criticism can leave a fear of failure running well into adulthood. New work covered by science daily found those emotional patterns may be more flexible than they feel.

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:05.680
Young adults who revisited painful memories through imagery-based therapy showed lasting reductions in fear, guilt, sadness, and stress.

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:14.540
The method does not ask people to suppress the memory. It asks them to go back into it in imagery, and let the feeling attach to it change.

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:22.480
The sample is young adults, and the target is fear of failure, not a formal anxiety diagnosis, and not an older cohort.

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:28.960
Durability beyond the study window, and a test in people who meet clinical criteria are still open.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:37.580
You've probably heard that memories are stored like files in a cabinet. Right once, close the drawer, pull the same folder out later, unchanged.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:49.520
That picture is wrong. The brain does not file a finished object. It stores a trace that has to be rebuilt every time you use it, and the act of rebuilding briefly makes the trace unstable.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:57.960
Neuroscientists call the first locking down consolidation. They call the later, recall-triggered reopening, re-consolidation.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:06.920
Right now, as you picture the room you are sitting in, or the last conversation that stung, the networks holding that episode are temporarily writable.

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:19.000
New proteins have to be made to lock the trace back down. If that protein-making step is blocked in the laboratory, the classic demonstration used a protein synthesis inhibitor in the amygdala of rats,

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:25.680
after a single reminder of a learned shock, a fear memory that had already been consolidated can fail to return.

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:41.620
The open window is often put at about four to six hours. That is long enough for a conversation, a therapy session, or a night of rumination to rewrite the feeling attached to the event, and short enough that by the next day, the updated version is what remains.

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:51.180
The conditions for opening the trace are stricter than a casual recollection. A strong old memory does not always unlock just because you thought of it.

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:58.380
Many experiments suggest the brain needs a mismatch, a prediction error, before it treats the trace as worth updating.

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:07.600
That is one reason a vivid, structured retelling can change a fear, while idly turning the same scene over in your head can cut the groove deeper.

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:16.940
Unstructured replay often restates the old prediction, so the window never really opens, or it opens and then restabilizes the same emotion.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:23.840
Imagery-based work, including the fear of failure study just mentioned, tries to use that window on purpose.

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:36.520
The person is asked to re-enter the scene in enough sensory detail that the memory comes online, then to let a different ending, a different body state, or a different meaning attached before the proteins lock it again.

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:51.980
None of this means every painful memory should be poked, boundary conditions matter, very recent trauma, dissociation, and some medications that interfere with protein synthesis or attention can change whether reconsolidation is even in play.

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:59.360
If you are going to recall something difficult, how and with whom you do it is part of the biology, not a side issue.

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:06.300
A structured session with someone who knows the method is a different intervention from replaying the insult at one in the morning.

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:18.880
Watch for trials that test whether reconsolidation-based method still hold months later in older adults and in diagnosed anxiety, not only at the end of a short protocol in young volunteers.

00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:23.640
That durability test is how you tell a rewritten trace from a good week.

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:31.500
From how a living brain rewrites a feeling to how an extinct one forces a rewrite of something we thought we knew about bodies.

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:45.000
A new study by the Department of Anatomy and Anthropology at Tel Aviv University, published in scientific reports, takes up one of the striking differences between men and women, how the modern human pelvis evolved.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:56.620
Male and Neanderthal pelvis resemble those of modern females. That comparison challenges decades old assumptions about which pelvic shapes count as male or female in our evolutionary past.

00:06:57.340 --> 00:07:03.840
The argument is built from fossil anatomy set against living human pelvic form, not from a genetic reconstruction.

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:10.040
Additional fossil measurements will show whether the resemblance holds beyond the specimens used in this paper.

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:18.140
A finding about deep time, then a finding about right now. A plant product and a poison center curve that got away from the guidance.

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:25.140
Cratum use and serious health problems linked to it have risen dramatically across the United States over the past decade.

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:32.340
Poison Center reports jumped from 19 cases in 2010 to more than 1,223.

00:07:33.340 --> 00:07:39.420
Severe outcomes including life-threatening complications, disability and death also climbed sharply.

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:50.200
Researchers warned that Cratum can trigger seizures, abnormal heart rhythms, liver damage and breathing problems, with risks potentially increasing when it is combined with other drugs.

00:07:50.780 --> 00:07:58.320
The case counts come from poison center reports, which capture people who were ill enough for someone to call, not the full user population.

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:05.380
Labeling, scheduling or clinical guidance catching up with that volume is the public health question the numbers put on the table.

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:13.280
From a product already in wide use to two delivery ideas still on the bench, vehicles that might someday carry a therapy.

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:22.820
Red blood cells serve as the foundation for nano-carriers that a new study tested as vehicles for gene therapy, tumor targeting and other medical treatments.

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:33.420
Scientists at the Ohio State University engineered extracellular vesicles on that template and showed that the vesicles could evade immune cells and target cancer cells.

00:08:33.940 --> 00:08:37.080
Those are two jobs that delivery vehicles usually fail at.

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:40.640
The study is published in advanced healthcare materials.

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:44.760
What the paper establishes is a vehicle, not a finished medicine.

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:50.320
No therapeutic cargo is described as having completed an animal efficacy test here.

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:57.980
Loading a real payload and taking that construct into animals is the experiment that would connect the design to in vivo delivery.

00:08:58.580 --> 00:09:00.900
Another remote control idea for genes.

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:05.520
This one from outside of the body, with a sensor instead of a cell derived shell.

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:14.960
The DNA of living organisms contains regulatory elements that control when, where and to what extent specific genes are turned on or off.

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:18.520
Those elements can be co-opted to create gene switches.

00:09:18.980 --> 00:09:27.200
Tools for understanding gene expression and for therapeutic applications, particularly the non-invasive treatment or management of genetic disorders.

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:38.580
A new report, covered by Fizz.org, describes a molecular electromagnetic sensor that may enable remote control gene therapy using those switches from outside the body.

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:45.540
External electromagnetic control is the proposed route, not a chemical inducer the patient has to take.

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:53.520
Demonstration in living tissue with a safety profile attached is what would move the sensor from a laboratory idea toward a therapy.

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:57.340
Delivery and control are the hopeful half of gene therapy.

00:09:57.860 --> 00:10:02.920
The other half is what happens when high-dose viral vectors go wrong and no one says so.

00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:09.640
Two recent patient deaths have drawn international scrutiny to China's investigator initiated trial framework.

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:13.840
The IIT path used as an alternative regulatory route.

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:22.540
Both deaths involved severe immune reactions to high-dose viral vector delivered gene therapies, and both came with a lack of disclosure.

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:29.760
The Fierce Biotech report describes what appeared to be systemic gaps in oversight and transparency on that path.

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:35.620
High-dose viral vectors are already known to be capable of triggering dangerous immune responses.

00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:41.620
The added issue here is that the deaths were not disclosed in a way that satisfied outside observers.

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:46.220
Whether IIT disclosure rules change is the concrete follow-up.

00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:54.980
Next time, we'll be watching for whether those disclosure rules actually move, and for the paper that names how a resident T.Cell transduces touch.

